Could not refute — the divergence is real, stable across 5+ runs, three working directories, and `LC_ALL=C`, and is not any of the four accepted deviations (program name differs too, but the *message text itself* differs: "expected one argument" vs "invalid int value: '--'").

Root cause is in `/output/lib/argparse/parser.mjs`. `classify()` labels the bare `--` token itself as a positional:

```js
if (optionsEnded || token === '--') {
  optionsEnded = true;
  entries.push({ kind: 'positional', token });
```

and the value-consumption step then accepts any `kind === 'positional'` neighbour:

```js
} else if (i + 1 < classified.length && classified[i + 1].kind === 'positional') {
  raw = classified[i + 1].token;
```

CPython instead gives the bare `--` the pattern character `'-'` (only tokens *after* it become `'A'`), and `_match_argument` strips every `-` from the nargs pattern for optional actions (`'(-*A-*)'` → `'(A)'`), so a leading `'-'` can never match — hence `expected one argument`. The port needs `--` excluded from the set of tokens an optional may consume, while still remaining a leftover.

I also found the claim understates the impact: for `--c` (a `str` option, no int conversion to fail) the divergence is not stderr-only — exit code and error class differ, so `diff1.sh` fails even without `--stderr`.

Scope check (all confirmed by me): affected only when a bare `--` immediately follows an option expecting a value. `-- --a 1 ...` (leading), `... --c A --` and `... --c A -- x` (trailing), `--a=--` (attached), and `--a -` (single dash) all match the reference exactly.
